{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XbPyXrQ3QPYax6eiRiggF8","type":"tool_result","content":"**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is not scanned. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile and is not in the published `ard` image.\ntrufflehog 3.100.0\ninternal/redact/scan.go:34:func Builtin() Scanner { return builtin{} }\ninternal/scanner/loop.go:46:func NewLoop(p *Pipeline, builtins ...Builtin) *Loop { return &Loop{p: p, builtins: builtins} }\ninternal/scanner/loop.go:162:func (l *Loop) runBuiltin(ctx context.Context, env controller.Env, b Builtin) error {\ninternal/scanner/loop_test.go:38:func (b *fakeBuiltin) Name() string         { return b.name }\ninternal/scanner/loop_test.go:39:func (b *fakeBuiltin) Every() time.Duration { return b.every }\ninternal/scanner/loop_test.go:40:func (b *fakeBuiltin) Run(context.Context) error {\ninternal/scanner/loop_test.go:56:func newLoopFixture(t *testing.T, roles []config.Role, builtins ...Builtin) *loopFixture {\ninternal/scanner/loop_test.go:182:func TestLoopSkipsLLMScannersAndBuiltinCollisions(t *testing.T) {\ninternal/scanner/loop_test.go:196:func TestLoopRunsBuiltinsFromTheirOwnDatabase(t *testing.T) {\npackage roles\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/scanner\"\n)\n\n// thFake stands in for trufflehog: it records argv and replays stdout, stderr\n// and the exit code from files named by the environment.\nconst thFake = `#!/bin/sh\nif [ \"${1:-}\" = --version ]; then echo \"trufflehog 3.100.0\"; exit 0; fi\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$*\" >> \"$TH_ARGV\"\n[ -z \"${TH_STDOUT:-}\" ] || cat \"$TH_STDOUT\"\n[ -z \"${TH_STDERR:-}\" ] || cat \"$TH_STDERR\" >&2\nexit \"${TH_RC:-0}\"\n`\n\ntype thResult struct {\n\tstdout, stderr string\n\tcode           int\n\targv           string\n\ttmp            string\n}\npackage roles\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"reflect\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\ttag := fmt.Sprintf(\"ard-lint-it:%d\", time.Now().UnixNano())\n\tif _, se, code := dockerOut(t, 20*time.Minute, \"build\", \"-q\", \"-t\", tag, \"scanners/lint\"); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"docker build: %s\", se)\n\t}\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { dockerOut(t, time.Minute, \"image\", \"rm\", tag) })\n\trole := lintRoleCopy(t, strp(\"\"))\n\n\trepo := t.TempDir()\n\tlintFixtureModule(t, repo)\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(repo, \"go.mod\"), []byte(\"module example.com/lintfixture\\n\\ngo 1.27\\n\"), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tout, se, code := dockerOut(t, 20*time.Minute, scanRunArgs(tag, repo, role)...)\n\tif code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit %d\\n%s\", code, se)\n\t}\n\tfor _, w := range []string{\"lint:errcheck:main.go:\", \"lint:govet/printf:main.go:\", \"lint:unused:main.go:\", \"lint:nolint:main.go:\"} {\n\t\tif !strings.Contains(out, w) {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"missing %s in:\\n%s\", w, out)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(repo, \"go.mod\"), []byte(\"module example.com/lintfixture\\n\\ngo 1.99\\n\"), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tout, se, code = dockerOut(t, 20*time.Minute, scanRunArgs(tag, repo, role)...)\n\tif code != 1 || out != \"\" || !strings.Contains(se, \"go.mod needs a newer Go than the image's\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"go 1.99: exit %d stdout %q\\n%s\", code, out, se)\n\t}\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"674570e0-cf8a-493c-8777-d3d2c16c66e6","uuid":"fd141765-7295-4fa3-afa9-9514b8179cf0","timestamp":"2026-11-23T22:50:08.870Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: need